end of day4 -Today I refined the NEAT car racing setup, making the simulation loop stable and efficient. I cleaned up car_env_neat.py, added culling for slow agents, and ensured cars keep moving until they crash instead of idling. I fixed a crash caused by max() when all cars died by restructuring alive-car tracking, and tuned car.py for smoother acceleration, speed, and sensor range. The system now runs cleanly across generations with clear progress tracking. Main issues left are reward tuning and balancing speed for more consistent full-track driving.
Today I went back into my older version of the project because I wanted to add improvements I thought would push the learning further — things like extra sensors, a better reward system, new collision logic, and more control outputs.

But instead of improving it, everything basically collapsed.

I kept trying to “upgrade” little parts (collision, rotation logic, sensor directions, reward shaping, NEAT outputs, new actions) without realising how connected everything was.
Changing one thing broke three others. Then trying to fix those broke even more.

Eventually:

Cars stopped moving properly

Collision became inconsistent

NEAT wouldn’t run

Reward system made no sense

Config files went out of sync

My NEAT library was a weird broken fork

The environment loop kept crashing

The entire project became unfixable

At that point I realised the project wasn’t “bad” — it was just built on too many patches.
Nothing was clean anymore.

So Day 5 was mostly the realisation that the old version couldn’t be saved in a clean way.
Trying to fix it just caused more breakage.
It taught me that sometimes restarting from a clean base is faster than trying to repair a tangled mess.
